A||||4||extinct||2 problems with the gas gauge ||||8||
Z||000000||extinct||01-19-2001||04:01 AM||||I am having problems with my gas gauge.<P>   When the gas tank is between 1/2 and full<BR>the gauge likes to peg all the way to the right (past full).  It does not do it all the time but maybee 50% of the time and only when the tank is 1/2 to full. When it is not peged, it reads about 1/4 to much.<P>  When the gas tank is between empty and 1/2 full the gauge will read 1/4 to much, but it does not peg at this fuel level, just reads to much.<P>I have searched the forum and have found many threads about the gauge reading to much.  Many threads talk about the peged temp gauge, but I could not find anything about the INTERMITTENT PEGGING GAS GAUGE!<P>I suspect the sending unit for the reading to high.  But what about the pegging?? How about a bad ground?<P>Its a low mileage 88gt.<P>Thanks guys/gals.<p>[This message has been edited by extinct (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-19-2001||03:12 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>I too suspected my sending unit was going bad when my gauge started to act up.  Not the same problem as yours, but before you go through the hassle of dropping the tank, you can test the gauge easily.<P>All you need to do is make a tester circut.  I could explain it here, but I will give credit to it's inventor, Oliver Scholtz.  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tester.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tester.html</A> <P>I have used my tester that I built with his instructions a lot, and I found out with it that the "calibration resistor" that is on the back of the gauge itself was bad; it had some corrosion on the terminals so it wasn't doing its job.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||01-19-2001||10:58 PM|||| pointer is lose on pivit. open cover glass and remove pointer, fill tank and glue on at full mark. did it after it ran out of gas at 1/2 full.used super glue one drop .<BR>works fine now    ray||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-19-2001||11:36 PM||||Intermitant. 1/2 tank.<P>I'd be more inclined to think a wire is pinching or the sender is bad.<P>Why a pinch... well when the tank is low it may not pull hard enough to pinch the wire. When it's full the thing sticks in place. <P>as a temporary test. Try stuffing some wood or plastic between the tank and various places so it can't posibly move.<P>If the problem stops the wiring is suspect.<P>either way the tank has to come down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||extinct||01-20-2001||12:13 AM||||Thanks for the advice.  I did build the gauge<BR>testing thing 0 ohm / 90 ohm and will test the gauge first.  One thing I forgot is that when the tank is between 1/2 and full, the gauge needle jumps around alot between peged and 1/2. There is alot of needle movement as the gas is moving around in the tank (corner and hills caused this) It does not do this when below 1/2 but it is still reading to much (about 1/4 tank to much)<P>This months FOCOA has a brief diagnosis of the fuel gauge/sending units in it.  But I seem to have both the inacuarte gage reading and the pegging gauge.<P>Thanks again.||||reg||1
